    "knowledge_point": "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector",
    "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel.",
    "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =c(2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =2c{e}^{2t}\\,i+c(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}=2c{e}^{2t} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2}=c(2t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting $c=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2t+1) \\\\ {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the point corresponding to $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & ={e}^{0}\\,i+({0}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$",
